---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import encryptedPhilosophy from '../data/philosophy.encrypted.json';
import privateCollectionModuleUrl from '../lib/private-collection.js?url';

// Collections on one page, picked with the tab bar: `quotes` (Ideas),
// `history` (History), `cognitiveScience` (Cognitive Science), and two private tabs.
// Private collections are authored in ignored .private/ JSON files, then encrypted locally.
// Only ciphertext ships here. The browser decrypts it after the password is entered.
// All collections use the Entry shape below and the same card.
// An IDEA is a quotation: { text: '...', author: '...', source?: '...' }. Order is
// whatever reads best, since the list is shuffled on load anyway.
// A HISTORY entry is a short retold story: { text: [...], subject: '...', year: '...' }.
// A COGNITIVE SCIENCE entry is an explained concept: { text: [...], title: '...' }.
// `subject` or `title` in place of `author` switches the card over: the body renders as
// prose rather than a blockquote, and its heading sits above it instead of an em-dash
// attribution below. History and Cognitive Science are NOT shuffled, so array order is
// display order.
// `text` can be an array when several passages from the same person and occasion
// belong together: each becomes its own paragraph under one attribution. The paragraph
// break IS the elision mark: when a talk is stitched from passages with the connective
// material cut, split at every cut and let the gap show it. Do not write ellipses at the
// seams (…like this…) and do not join two passages inside one paragraph; the ellipses
// only add visual noise to something the layout already says, and they read as the
// speaker trailing off. Set `paraphrased` alongside, so the card says so in words too.
// `source` is optional context (a book, talk, or paper) shown after the name, and
// links to `url` when one is given. Set `paraphrased` when the wording is recalled
// rather than transcribed, so the page never passes it off as verbatim.
// `note` is your own commentary on the excerpt. It renders below the attribution,
// under a rule and in a lighter register, so it reads as annotation and never as
// something the speaker said. A line is the usual size; pass an array when it needs
// more than one paragraph, and only the first gets the rule. Like `text` it goes
// through set:html, so it can carry an inline link.
// `image` hangs a figure from the same source under the quote. Keep it grayscale
// line art: dark mode inverts the image rather than punching a white slab into the
// card, which is lossless for black-on-white and wrong for anything colored.
// Set `plain` when the figure is colored but on a white background: it keeps the
// multiply/invert off so the art renders as-is, which costs a white slab in dark mode
// but is the same trade the white-background note figures already make, and is much
// better than an invert that hue-flips salmon to teal. `credit`/`creditUrl` add a
// figcaption, for when the figure comes from somewhere other than the quote itself.
// `width` caps one figure below the shared 460px, for art that is tall or square and
// would otherwise eat the whole card. It keeps the min(100%, …) form so a phone still
// wins on the 100% side.
// `svg` is the escape hatch for a source figure that is neither: a colored or
// dark-background diagram would come out hue-flipped under that invert, so redraw it
// as inline SVG on the --diagram-*-border tokens instead. It is injected with
// `set:html`, which means Astro's scoped styles never reach it, so its sizing and
// colors live inline on the element (and `var()` needs `style=`, not a presentation
// attribute, to resolve).
// Every entry gets a shareable #anchor, slugged from `author`, `subject`, or `title`. A second
// entry under the same name fails the build; give that one an explicit `id` (short and
// memorable, e.g. id: 'karpathy-understanding') so the first one's shared link keeps
// working. The check spans all collections, and every collection key is reserved: the
// script reads those as "open that panel".
// Redrawn from the chart in the HubSpot post credited on the figure: that original is
// a dark-navy branded card, which the .quote-figure invert would turn cream-on-pink.
const aiSingularityChart = `<svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="ai-sing-title" style="display:block;width:min(100%,460px);height:auto;margin:0 auto">
  <title id="ai-sing-title">Intelligence plotted against time: a gently rising human curve, and an AI curve that starts lower, crosses it, and then climbs steeply.</title>
  <defs>
    <marker id="ai-sing-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" orient="auto-start-reverse">
      <path d="M 0 1 L 9 5 L 0 9 z" style="fill: var(--diagram-orange-border)" />
    </marker>
  </defs>
  <path d="M 70 26 L 70 200 L 392 200" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" style="stroke: var(--text-muted)" />
  <path d="M 70 185 Q 230 162 380 128" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" style="stroke: currentColor" />
  <path d="M 70 196 C 190 194 265 180 305 144 C 340 104 365 58 380 32" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" style="stroke: var(--diagram-blue-border)" />
  <path d="M 196 78 Q 250 96 285 126" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" marker-end="url(#ai-sing-arrow)" style="stroke: var(--diagram-orange-border)" />
  <circle cx="305" cy="144" r="5.5" style="fill: var(--diagram-orange-border)" />
  <text x="388" y="36" font-size="13" style="fill: var(--diagram-blue-border)">AI</text>
  <text x="388" y="132" font-size="13" style="fill: currentColor">Human</text>
  <text x="231" y="230" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" style="fill: var(--text-muted)">Time</text>
  <text x="50" y="113" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90 50 113)" style="fill: var(--text-muted)">Intelligence</text>
</svg>`;

type Entry = {
  text: string | string[];
  // `author` marks an idea: the body renders as a quote, attributed with an em dash.
  // `subject` marks a history entry instead: the body renders as prose, and the
  // person and `year` the story is about sit above it rather than below. `title`
  // likewise marks an explained concept in another collection. The slug comes from
  // whichever of the three is set.
  author?: string;
  subject?: string;
  title?: string;
  year?: string;
  source?: string;
  sourceBold?: boolean;
  url?: string;
  paraphrased?: boolean;
  note?: string | string[];
  image?: { src: string; alt: string; plain?: boolean; credit?: string; creditUrl?: string; width?: number };
  // ISO date this went up. Everything added from Sep 2026 on carries one.
  added?: string;
  svg?: { markup: string; credit?: string; creditUrl?: string };
  id?: string;
};

const quotes: Entry[] = [
  {
    id: 'plato-appearance-of-wisdom',
    text: 'You offer your pupils the appearance of wisdom, not true wisdom, for they will ' +
      'read many things without instruction and will therefore seem to know many things, ' +
      'when they are for the most part ignorant and hard to get along with, since they are ' +
      'not wise, but only appear wise.',
    author: 'Plato',
    source: 'Phaedrus, 275a–b',
    url: 'https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0174%3Atext%3DPhaedrus%3Apage%3D275',
    added: '2026-10-03',
    note: [
      'Socrates’ fable warns about the illusion of intelligence caused by the accumulation ' +
        'of written knowledge.',
      'In his <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/posts/yann-lecun_socrates-on-the-invention-of-writing-and-activity-7512137592616677378-vg24" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn post</a>, ' +
        'Yann LeCun writes: “Little did he know that his warning would apply to LLMs ' +
        '(in addition to some categories of humans). So many people confuse the accumulation ' +
        'of declarative knowledge by LLMs with super-human intelligence.”',
    ],
  },
  {
    text: 'The most effect in the AI age could probably made at either extreme: either close ' +
      'to the technology, actually making LLMs, or close to the customer, using AI to give ' +
      'them exactly what they want. Or maybe both if you can stretch that far.',
    author: 'Paul Graham',
    source: 'on X',
    url: 'https://x.com/AGTPinsights/status/2102717569434341642',
    paraphrased: true,
    added: '2026-09-30',
  },
  {
    text: [
      'Amtrak made a lot of fanfare about its fancy new trains, which can reach 160 mph. But ' +
        'the limiting factor is the country’s outdated track infrastructure, so actual speeds ' +
        'have barely changed and still average around 65 mph.',
      'AI is similar. The LLM is a fast new train, but the real-world systems it runs through ' +
        'are old tracks. Upgrading them will not happen overnight because the bottlenecks are ' +
        'political, economic, and sociological.',
    ],
    author: 'Arvind Narayanan',
    source: 'on AI adoption',
    paraphrased: true,
    added: '2026-09-30',
  },
  {
    text: [
      'Most people never say what they want. It’s not because we don’t want. We want fiercely ' +
        'and constantly. It happens in the moments before falling asleep, while daydreaming, ' +
        'or on the drive home. But rarely do we get to express these dreams, and even more ' +
        'rarely do they ever come true.',
      'When we can’t name our dreams, we mostly can’t reach them. The world is a maze of ' +
        'obstacles, and most dreams die long before they are said aloud. They die in the ' +
        'uncertainty of where to start and in the phone calls never made.',
      'Every unrealized want is a quiet tragedy of human potential.',
      'Now imagine every person on Earth with a second mind beyond their own, whose sole job ' +
        'is to discover what they want and help make it happen. It hears the half-sentence, ' +
        '“I feel like there’s something more I could be doing,” and helps fill in the blanks. ' +
        'It keeps them on schedule, clears the way, and solves all the problems it can until ' +
        'the human part, the wanting and the dreaming, is all that’s left.',
      'We have no idea what people will want. Until now, the world has been shaped by the small ' +
        'number of fanatics who have somehow found a way to make their wants real. But we’ve ' +
        'never seen humanity with every single person’s agency fully switched on: eight billion ' +
        'people who need only write down their dream to begin making it happen.',
    ],
